Lab 56.2 --- tunnel-mode ESP overhead, computed from the frame formats
========================================================================

Path MTU assumed: 1500 bytes (the ordinary Ethernet case).

  transform                      NAT-T   overhead inner MTU  TCP MSS
  aes-gcm-256                    no            54      1446     1406
  aes-gcm-256                    yes           62      1438     1398
  chacha20-poly1305              no            54      1446     1406
  chacha20-poly1305              yes           62      1438     1398
  aes-cbc-256+hmac-sha1-96       no            58      1438     1398
  aes-cbc-256+hmac-sha1-96       yes           66      1422     1382
  aes-cbc-256+hmac-sha256-128    no            62      1438     1398
  aes-cbc-256+hmac-sha256-128    yes           70      1422     1382

Why this is a search and not a subtraction. ESP pads the ciphertext to the
cipher's boundary, so several different inner sizes produce the SAME total:

    inner 1444  ->  total 1500   <- the largest that fits
    inner 1445  ->  total 1500   <- the largest that fits
    inner 1446  ->  total 1500   <- the largest that fits
    inner 1447  ->  total 1504   (too big)
    inner 1448  ->  total 1504   (too big)
    inner 1449  ->  total 1504   (too big)
    inner 1450  ->  total 1504   (too big)
    inner 1451  ->  total 1508   (too big)

MACsec, for comparison, is link-layer and its cost lands on the
Ethernet frame: SecTAG 16 bytes with SCI (8 without) plus a 16-byte
ICV, so 32 bytes in the common case. A switch that cannot carry an
oversized frame passes that cost straight on to the IP MTU.

An MSS clamp set from the inner MTU protects TCP and only TCP.
